/* book: Q sets voltage; P = (VS VR / X) sin δ; TCR = L + two anti-parallel thyristors; BSVC = BC − BL(α); STATCOM holds current at low voltage */
{s:2,k:'notebook',term:'Key ideas',title:'Four ideas about SVCs',say:`Reactive power does no useful work, but it sets the voltage. A TCR is a reactor with two anti-parallel thyristors; fire later, and it takes fewer vars. Add a fixed capacitor, and the net swings from lagging to leading, a tidy tug of war. And a ${P('STATCOM')} holds its current even as the voltage sags.`,cues:{i1:'Reactive power does',i2:'A TCR is',i3:'Add a fixed capacitor',i3b:'a tidy tug',i4:'And a STATCOM'},items:[['Reactive power Q sets bus voltage and power transfer','i1'],['TCR: reactor + anti-parallel thyristors, α from 90° to 180°','i2'],['TCR + fixed capacitor: from lagging to leading','i3'],['STATCOM: a VSC that holds its current as voltage sags','i4']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'i1'),PE_box(14,10,612,152)+PE_T(34,44,'1 · Q sets the bus voltage',{fs:24,fw:700})+PE_T(40,96,`P = (${PE_sub('V','S')} ${PE_sub('V','R')} / X) sin δ`,{fs:28,fw:800,fill:BLU})+PE_T(40,142,'absorb Q: V falls',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG})+PE_T(330,142,'supply Q: V rises',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i2'),PE_box(14,182,612,152)+PE_T(34,216,'2 · TCR: reactor + two thyristors',{fs:24,fw:700})+PE_dot(50,280)+PE_T(42,322,'bus',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:PE_MUT})+PE_L(50,280,110,280,PE_INK,3)+PE_pair(150,280,ORG)+PE_L(190,280,206,280,PE_INK,3)+PE_coil(206,330,280,BLU,4)+PE_L(330,280,350,280,PE_INK,3)+PE_L(350,280,350,300,PE_INK,3)+PE_gnd(350,300)+PE_T(384,268,'α = 90°: full',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:BLU})+PE_T(384,304,'α = 180°: off',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:PE_MUT}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i3'),PE_box(14,354,612,152)+PE_T(34,388,'3 · net = capacitor − TCR',{fs:24,fw:700})+PE_L(90,452,570,452,PE_AX,3)+PE_L(330,440,330,464,PE_MUT,3)+`<rect x="${(330-48.9*4).toFixed(1)}" y="446" width="${(108.9*4).toFixed(1)}" height="12" rx="6" fill="${GRN}"/>`+PE_T(134,490,'48.9 lagging',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG})+PE_T(330,490,'0',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:PE_MUT})+PE_T(570,490,'60 leading',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i3b'),PE_arrow(340,420,470,420,BLU,5)+PE_arrow(320,420,190,420,ORG,5)+PE_T(480,428,'C',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU})+PE_T(180,428,'TCR',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'end',fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i4'),PE_box(14,526,612,154)+PE_T(34,560,'4 · low voltage: who keeps current?',{fs:24,fw:700})+PE_L(80,664,80,580,PE_AX,3)+PE_L(80,664,330,664,PE_AX,3)+PE_T(70,596,'V',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:PE_MUT,it:1})+PE_T(340,670,'I',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:PE_MUT,it:1})+PE_L(80,664,300,594,ORG,5)+PE_L(300,594,300,648,GRN,5)+PE_T(360,610,'SVC: I falls with V',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG})+PE_T(360,646,'STATCOM: I held',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN}))}`},
/* book Worked example (chapter 6.5): 33 kV, XL = 10 Ω, α = 120° and 90° (checked in python: BL = 0.03910 S, 745.0 A, 42.58 MVAr; 0.1 S, 1905 A, 108.9 MVAr) */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2",title:"Worked example 2",say:'Example two: a TCR, ten ohms per phase, on a thirty-three kilovolt bus. At one hundred and twenty degrees, the bracket is one point two two eight. Divide by pi times ten: zero point zero three nine one siemens. The phase voltage is nineteen thousand and fifty-two volts, so seven hundred and forty-five amps flow.',cues:{fk:'Example two',k1:'At one hundred',k1b:'one point two',k2:'Divide by pi',k2b:'zero point zero',k3:'The phase voltage',k3b:'so seven hundred'},q:[`A 33 kV, 50 Hz bus has a star TCR, ${PE_sub('X','L')} = 10 Ω per phase.`,'Find the fundamental current and Q at α = 120°, then at α = 90°.'],f:[`${PE_sub('B','L')}(α) = [2(π − α) + sin 2α] / (π ${PE_sub('X','L')})`,`I = ${PE_sub('B','L')} ${PE_sub('V','phase')},   Q = √3 ${PE_sub('V','LL')} I`],hint:'Radians: 120° = 2.094 rad; sin 240° = −0.866.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Bracket at 120°", ["2(1.047) + (−0.866)","2(π − α) + sin 2α = 1.228"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Susceptance", [`${PE_sub('B','L')} = 1.228 / (π × 10)`,`${PE_sub('B','L')}(120°) = 0.0391 S`], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Phase current", [`${PE_sub('V','phase')} = 33 kV / √3 = 19,052 V`,"I = 0.0391 × 19,052 = 745 A"], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Reactive power", ["Q = √3 × 33 kV × 0.745 kA","Q(120°) = 42.6 MVAr"], ["k4","k4b"]], ["At α = 90°", [`${PE_sub('B','L')} = π / (π × 10) = 0.1 S`,"I = 1905 A,  Q(90°) = 108.9 MVAr"], ["k5","k5b"]]],live:[0,1,2],rh:84},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2 (vars)",title:"Worked example 2",say:'Now the vars: root three times thirty-three kilovolts times zero point seven four five kiloamps, forty-two point six mega vars. At ninety degrees, the susceptance is one over ten: zero point one siemens, so nineteen hundred and five amps, and one hundred and eight point nine mega vars.',pause:1,cues:{fk:'Now the vars',k4:'root three times',k4b:'forty-two point six',k5:'At ninety degrees',k5b:'so nineteen hundred',a:'and one hundred'},q:[`A 33 kV, 50 Hz bus has a star TCR, ${PE_sub('X','L')} = 10 Ω per phase.`,'Find the fundamental current and Q at α = 120°, then at α = 90°.'],f:[`${PE_sub('B','L')}(α) = [2(π − α) + sin 2α] / (π ${PE_sub('X','L')})`,`I = ${PE_sub('B','L')} ${PE_sub('V','phase')},   Q = √3 ${PE_sub('V','LL')} I`],hint:'Radians: 120° = 2.094 rad; sin 240° = −0.866.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Bracket at 120°", ["2(1.047) + (−0.866)","2(π − α) + sin 2α = 1.228"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["Susceptance", [`${PE_sub('B','L')} = 1.228 / (π × 10)`,`${PE_sub('B','L')}(120°) = 0.0391 S`], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Phase current", [`${PE_sub('V','phase')} = 33 kV / √3 = 19,052 V`,"I = 0.0391 × 19,052 = 745 A"], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Reactive power", ["Q = √3 × 33 kV × 0.745 kA","Q(120°) = 42.6 MVAr"], ["k4","k4b"]], ["At α = 90°", [`${PE_sub('B','L')} = π / (π × 10) = 0.1 S`,"I = 1905 A,  Q(90°) = 108.9 MVAr"], ["k5","k5b"]]],live:[3,4],rh:84,ans:['120°: 745 A, 42.6 MVAr; 90°: 1905 A, 108.9 MVAr','a']},
/* book Worked example 2 (chapter 6.5): TCR + 60 MVAr capacitor (checked: −48.9, +60, ratio 0.5510, 110° → 0.5732, 112° → 0.5344, root 111.14°) */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3",title:"Worked example 3",say:'Example three: add a sixty mega var capacitor. At ninety degrees, sixty minus one hundred and eight point nine leaves forty-eight point nine lagging. At one eighty, the reactor is off: sixty leading. Zero output needs a ratio of zero point five five one: about one hundred and eleven degrees.',pause:1,cues:{fk:'Example three',k1:'At ninety degrees',k1b:'leaves forty-eight',k2:'At one eighty',k2b:'sixty leading',k3:'Zero output needs',k3b:'zero point five',k4:'about one hundred',k4b:'eleven degrees',a:'eleven degrees'},q:[`The TCR above (${PE_sub('X','L')} = 10 Ω, 33 kV) works with a 60 MVAr fixed capacitor.`,'Find the range of the SVC output and the α for zero output.'],f:[`net output = 60 − ${PE_sub('Q','L')}(α)   (leading +)`,`${PE_sub('Q','L')}(α) / ${PE_sub('Q','L')}(90°) = [2(π − α) + sin 2α] / π`],hint:`${PE_sub('Q','L')}(90°) = 108.9 MVAr from worked example 2.`,fk:'fk',steps:[["α = 90°", ["60 − 108.9 = −48.9 MVAr","48.9 MVAr lagging"], ["k1","k1b"]], ["α = 180°", [`${PE_sub('Q','L')} = 0: no conduction`,"60 MVAr leading"], ["k2","k2b"]], ["Zero output", [`${PE_sub('Q','L')} = 60, ratio = 60 / 108.9`,"ratio = 0.551"], ["k3","k3b"]], ["Trial", ["110°: 0.573 (high);  112°: 0.534 (low)","α ≈ 111° gives 0.551"], ["k4","k4b"]]],live:[0,1,2,3],ans:['48.9 lagging to 60 leading; zero at α ≈ 111°','a']},
/* book trap: taking the TCR output as the SVC output (net = BC − BL(α)) */
{s:2,k:'trap2',term:'Exam trap',title:'Exam trap!',say:'Here is the trap. At ninety degrees, students quote the reactor\'s one hundred and eight point nine mega vars as the SVC output. But the capacitor supplies sixty. The net is the difference: forty-eight point nine lagging.',cues:{x:'Here is the trap',w:'students quote the',r:'But the capacitor'},wl:"Don't write",rl:'Write',wrong:['At α = 90°, SVC output = TCR vars','= 108.9 MVAr lagging'],right:['Net = capacitor − TCR = 60 − 108.9','= −48.9: 48.9 MVAr lagging'],wv:(c,u,b)=>`
${PE_L(40,110,600,110,PE_INK,3)}${PE_bar(250,70,-108.9,ORG)}${PE_T(285,94,'TCR −108.9',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG})}${PE_T(350,220,'SVC = 108.9?',{fs:28,fw:800,fill:TRED})}${PE_T(36,94,'leading +',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:PE_MUT})}`,rv:(c,u,b)=>`
${PE_L(30,110,610,110,PE_INK,3)}${PE_bar(60,70,60,BLU)}${PE_T(95,140,'C +60',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})}${PE_bar(250,70,-108.9,ORG)}${PE_T(285,94,'TCR −108.9',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG})}${PE_bar(470,70,-48.9,GRN)}${PE_T(505,94,'net −48.9',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})}${PE_T(505,226,'48.9 lagging',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})}`},
/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:`Recap in three. HVDC power is the pole-to-pole voltage times the pole current, and either pole can run alone. A TCR with a fixed capacitor moves smoothly from lagging to leading. And a ${P('STATCOM')} holds its current as the voltage sags.`,cues:{r1:'HVDC power is',r2:'A TCR with',r3:'And a STATCOM'},panels:[["HVDC","BLU",'peRecLink',['DC power = pole-to-pole V × pole I','One pole out: half the power stays'],'r1'],["SVC","ORG",'peRecSvc',['Net = capacitor − TCR vars','90°: most lagging; 180°: leading'],'r2'],["STATCOM","GRN",'peRecStat',['Holds its current as voltage sags','An SVC current falls with voltage'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice: the book's own questions and model answers ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"What is the difference between a TSC and a TCSC?",say:'Question one. What is the difference between a TSC and a TCSC?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'What is the'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"What is the difference between a TSC and a TCSC?",say:'A TSC is a shunt thyristor-switched capacitor, used in an SVC for stepwise leading vars. A TCSC is a series thyristor-controlled capacitor, which varies the line\'s series reactance.',cues:{a1:'A TSC is',a2:'A TCSC is'},al:[["TSC: a shunt thyristor-switched capacitor, used in an SVC for stepwise leading vars.",'a1'],["TCSC: a series thyristor-controlled capacitor that varies the line series reactance.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),PE_L(30,70,590,70,PE_INK,4)+PE_T(30,52,'bus',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:PE_MUT})+PE_dot(170,70)+PE_L(170,70,170,92,PE_INK,3)+`<g transform="rotate(90 170 132)">${PE_pair(170,132,BLU)}</g>`+PE_L(170,172,170,196,PE_INK,3)+PE_capV(170,196,236,BLU)+PE_L(170,236,170,246,PE_INK,3)+PE_gnd(170,246)+PE_T(240,140,'TSC: shunt,',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU})+PE_T(240,176,'switched in steps',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:BLU})+PE_T(240,208,'leading vars only',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:PE_MUT}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),PE_T(30,302,'line',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:PE_MUT})+PE_L(30,400,200,400,PE_INK,4)+PE_cap(200,440,400,ORG,4)+PE_L(440,400,590,400,PE_INK,4)+PE_L(200,400,200,330,PE_INK,3)+PE_L(440,400,440,330,PE_INK,3)+PE_L(200,330,226,330,PE_INK,3)+PE_pair(266,330,ORG)+PE_L(306,330,316,330,PE_INK,3)+PE_coil(316,440,330,ORG,4)+PE_T(310,452,'TCSC: in series with the line',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A ±600 kV bipolar link delivers 3000 MW. Find the pole current and the power left if one pole trips with ground return.",say:'Question two. Take [a](/ɐ/) plus and minus six hundred kilovolt bipolar link, delivering three thousand megawatts. Find the pole current, and the power left if one pole trips.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'Take a plus'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A ±600 kV bipolar link delivers 3000 MW. Find the pole current and the power left if one pole trips with ground return.",say:'Pole to pole is twelve hundred kilovolts. Three thousand megawatts over that gives twenty-five hundred amps in each pole. One pole alone: six hundred kilovolts times that current, fifteen hundred megawatts.',cues:{fk:'Pole to pole',k1:'Pole to pole',k1b:'twelve hundred kilovolts',k2:'Three thousand megawatts',k2b:'twenty-five hundred amps',k3:'One pole alone',k3b:'fifteen hundred megawatts'},calc:{f:[`${PE_sub('P','d')} = ${PE_sub('V','d')} × ${PE_sub('I','d')}`,`${PE_sub('V','d')} = 2 × pole-to-ground`],hint:`One pole with ground return: P = 600 kV × ${PE_sub('I','d')}.`,fk:'fk',steps:[["Pole-to-pole voltage",[`${PE_sub('V','d')} = 2 × 600 kV`,`${PE_sub('V','d')} = 1200 kV`],["k1","k1b"]],["Pole current",[`${PE_sub('I','d')} = 3000 × 10⁶ / (1200 × 10³)`,`${PE_sub('I','d')} = 2500 A per pole`],["k2","k2b"]],["One pole left",["P = 600 × 10³ × 2500","P = 1500 MW"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Both stations of an LCC link absorb reactive power. Why does this not break energy balance?",say:'Question three. Both stations of a line-commutated link absorb reactive power. Why does this not break the energy balance?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'Both stations of'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Both stations of an LCC link absorb reactive power. Why does this not break energy balance?",say:'Reactive power is not real energy: it swings between the AC system and the converter twice per cycle. But it shifts the current away from the voltage, raising line current and loss, so filters, capacitors or synchronous condensers supply it locally.',cues:{a1:'Reactive power is',a2:'But it shifts',a2b:'so filters, capacitors'},al:[["Q is not real energy: it swings between the AC system and the converter twice per cycle.",'a1'],["It shifts the current from the voltage, raising line current and loss; filters, capacitors or synchronous condensers supply it locally.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),PE_box(20,40,210,80,{r:12,st:BLU,sw:3})+PE_T(125,90,'AC system',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+PE_box(390,40,210,80,{r:12,st:BLU,sw:3})+PE_T(495,90,'converter',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+PE_arrow(310,80,240,80,ORG,5)+PE_arrow(310,80,380,80,ORG,5)+PE_T(310,166,'Q swings, twice per cycle',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG})+PE_T(310,200,'net energy over a cycle: zero',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:PE_MUT}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),PE_arrow(60,320,300,320,BLU,5)+PE_T(310,328,'V',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU,it:1})+PE_arrow(60,320,215,430,ORG,5)+PE_T(226,446,'I',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:ORG,it:1})+`<path d="M150 320 A90 90 0 0 1 133 372" fill="none" stroke="${PE_MUT}" stroke-width="2"/>`+PE_T(40,268,'I shifted off V: more current, more loss',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:PE_INK}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2b'),PE_L(470,300,470,330,GRN,3)+PE_capV(470,330,380,GRN,4)+PE_L(470,380,470,396,GRN,3)+PE_gnd(470,396,GRN)+PE_T(470,456,'supplied locally',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the bipolar link and the reactor vars graph from memory, then check every number.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the bipolar'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["In this lesson", "HVDC transmission; Static VAR compensators and FACTS"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The ±500 kV bipolar link", "Each pole carries 2000 A; the two currents cancel in the earth, and either pole can run alone with earth return."], ["TCR vars against firing angle", "From 108.9 MVAr at 90° down to 0 at 180°; with the 60 MVAr capacitor the SVC runs from 48.9 MVAr lagging to 60 MVAr leading."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: Ned Mohan, Tore M. Undeland and William P. Robbins (Power Electronics: Converters, Applications and Design); Muhammad H. Rashid (Power Electronics: Devices, Circuits, and Applications); P. S. Bimbhra (Power Electronics)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:`So remember: DC power is the pole-to-pole voltage times the pole current, the SVC output is capacitor minus reactor, and a ${P('STATCOM')} holds its current. Next time, renewable energy and battery storage interfacing. See you there!`,cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'DC power is',w2:'the SVC output',w3:'and a STATCOM',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['V POLE TO POLE','BLU','w1'],['NET = C − TCR','ORG','w2'],['STATCOM HOLDS I','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Renewable energy and battery storage interfacing", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
